Otitis Media (Acute/Chronic)
Opthalmology & ENT
Bulging/erythematous tympanic membrane, Otalgia, Conductive hearing loss

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-031
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe, unilateral ear pain resulting in inconsolable distress and inability to lie flat.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient (child) and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an acutely distressed, febrile child exhibiting severe throbbing otalgia explicitly worsening in the supine position. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required to confirm tympanic membrane status, exclude impending spontaneous perforation, and rule out central intracranial complications (meningitis) or contiguous spread (mastoiditis) to prevent irreversible structural damage prior to routine general practice hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "They have been screaming and holding their left ear for the last six hours, and the crying gets much worse every time we try to put them in bed."
History of Present Illness: Condition commenced 48 hours prior with mild coryza and a subjective low-grade temperature. Evolved over the last 12 hours into severe unilateral throbbing ear pain (otalgia) that significantly worsens when the patient is in a supine position. Parent reports high fever, systemic irritability, and continuous tugging and pulling at the affected ear. Patient indicates a subjective sensation of deep aural fullness and muffled sound. Currently no otorrhea or sudden purulent ear discharge.
Functional Status: Highly agitated, refusing oral intake, completely unable to sleep.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver exhaustion; high risk of dehydration due to fluid refusal.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 | HR: 125 | Temp: 38.6 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 16 Kg
General: Febrile, flushed, continuously crying but consolable when held upright.
Physical Exam: Left otoscopy reveals a severely bulging, intensely erythematous, and completely opaque tympanic membrane with a distinct loss of the normal light reflex. Decreased tympanic membrane mobility noted clinically. Right tympanic membrane is mildly injected but normal in contour. Weber test lateralizes to the left ear, indicating conductive hearing loss. Neck is supple; negative Brudzinski and Kernig signs. Mastoid process is non-tender without erythema or bogginess.
Environmental Observations: Overheated bedroom with multiple blankets in the cot.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Intermittent, sub-therapeutic dosing of oral paracetamol.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Otitis Media - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Mastoiditis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of post-auricular erythema, swelling, or tenderness, and normal anatomical positioning of the pinna.

Otitis Externa - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of pain upon tragal palpation or pinna traction, and a visually clear external auditory canal.

Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Supple neck, absence of petechial/purpuric rash, normal tone, and the localized otological source entirely explaining the febrile state.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe, posture-dependent otalgia and high fever. The inability to sleep supine strongly indicated high-pressure middle ear effusion. Direct clinical evaluation definitively confirmed a severely bulging and erythematous tympanic membrane, necessitating immediate, weight-appropriate pharmacological intervention to prevent spontaneous membrane rupture. Given the severity of the symptoms and the risk of dehydration from distress-induced fluid refusal, definitive assessment and aggressive pain management could not be delayed until the next in-hours period. The urgent visit excluded mastoiditis, corrected analgesic under-dosing, and initiated targeted antimicrobial therapy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Amoxicillin 50mg/kg/day in divided doses for 5 days. Corrected weight-based dosing for oral Paracetamol and Ibuprofen to be given concurrently for immediate pain control.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage nocturnal pain and promote sleep, the caregiver must physically elevate the head of the child's mattress by placing a rolled towel underneath the mattress itself. The immediate mechanical reduction in middle ear venous pressure reduces the throbbing pain when the child is put down, positively reinforcing the positional modification and allowing the household to achieve sustained rest.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48-72 hours to ensure symptom resolution and assess membrane integrity.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the child develops a stiff neck, becomes increasingly lethargic or difficult to rouse, develops a non-blanching rash, or if redness and swelling appear behind the ear.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; assumes uncomplicated bacterial or viral middle ear effusion.
Clinical Course: Fever and severe pain should resolve within 48 hours of optimized analgesia and antibiotics. Fluid may remain behind the eardrum for weeks, causing temporary mild hearing reduction.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your child becomes extremely floppy, develops a rash that does not fade when you press a glass against it, or if the bone behind the ear swells up, call an ambulance immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: An infection has trapped fluid behind the eardrum, causing it to bulge outward. This pressure causes immense pain when lying flat. The antibiotics treat the infection, while the correct doses of pain relief keep the child comfortable enough to drink fluids.
Actionable Steps: Give pain medication exactly by the clock, do not wait for the child to wake up crying. Elevate the head of the bed. Discard heavy blankets to allow the fever to break naturally.

SMS INFORMATION

